/// The seven circulation events a notice can hang off.
///
/// Named `notice_trigger_kind` rather than `notice_trigger` because §3 already
/// has a TABLE called `notice_triggers`, which phase 22 owns. This is the enum
/// on `notice_policy_templates.trigger`.
enum NoticeTriggerKind {
  checkout
  checkin
  dueSoon
  overdue
  holdAvailable
  holdExpiring
  feeCharged

  @@map("notice_trigger_kind")
}

// -- Phase 14: patrons ---------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether a person may borrow, and why not.
///
/// 1.0's `MemberStatus` is `active | suspended | archived`, and `archived` there
/// is doing two jobs: it is a status AND it is the soft-delete `archivedAt`
/// column. Keeping both would let a row be `status = 'active'` with
/// `archived_at` set, which is a state no screen can render honestly. So
/// `archived` is NOT a status here — `archived_at` is the archive — and the
/// values that remain are the ones a librarian actually chooses between.
///
/// `expired` is derived from `expires_at` rather than stored, and is absent for
/// that reason: a status column that has to be swept nightly to stay true is a
/// column that is wrong every night until the sweep runs. Phase 16 compares the
/// instant.
enum PatronStatus {
  active
  /// A librarian's decision, with a reason in `patron_blocks`. Distinct from a
  /// BLOCK: a suspended patron is suspended for everything, whereas a block is
  /// per-reason and can be cleared one at a time.
  suspended
  /// The person asked to leave, or the library closed the account. Keeps the
  /// history; stops the borrowing.
  closed

  @@map("patron_status")
}

/// What a card is for, and whether it still opens the door.
enum PatronCardStatus {
  active
  /// Reported gone. The number stays on the row so a found card can be
  /// recognised as the one that was lost, rather than as an unknown barcode.
  lost
  stolen
  /// Superseded by a replacement. The commonest reason a patron has two rows.
  replaced
  expired

  @@map("patron_card_status")
}

/// The external identifier schemes a Greek library actually meets.
///
/// DELIBERATELY SHORT. §5 gives no list, and every value here is one a library
/// can point at on a document. `national_id` (ΑΔΤ), `tax_id` (ΑΦΜ) and
/// `amka` (the Greek social-security number) are the three a municipal library
/// is allowed to ask for; `student_id`, `staff_id` and `library_card` cover the
/// academic and consortium cases; `other` carries a label in `scheme_label` so
/// a library is never blocked on us adding a value.
///
/// AMKA IS SPECIAL-CATEGORY-ADJACENT under Greek law and is here so it can be
/// found and erased, not so it can be collected casually — `patron_identifiers`
/// is in the subject-access bundle for exactly this reason.
enum PatronIdentifierScheme {
  national_id
  tax_id
  amka
  passport
  student_id
  staff_id
  library_card
  other

  @@map("patron_identifier_scheme")
}

/// Which address, when a patron has more than one.
enum PatronAddressKind {
  home
  postal
  work
  /// Where a student actually sleeps in term time, which is the address an
  /// overdue notice has to reach.
  term_time
  other

  @@map("patron_address_kind")
}

/// How one patron stands to another.
///
/// Directional: the row means `from` IS THE `kind` OF `to`. `guardian` is the
/// one with legal weight — Greek L.4624/2019 art. 21 sets the digital-consent
/// age at 15, and phase 33 builds the double opt-in that needs this row to
/// already exist. Phase 14 stores the relationship and asserts nothing about
/// consent.
enum PatronRelationshipKind {
  guardian
  /// May borrow on the other's behalf. Distinct from `guardian`: an adult child
  /// collecting books for a housebound parent is a proxy and not a guardian.
  proxy
  household
  /// The academic case — a department or research group standing behind a
  /// reader.
  sponsor

  @@map("patron_relationship_kind")
}

/// Why a patron is blocked, and by whom.
///
/// TWO VOCABULARIES MEET HERE and the distinction is the whole design.
/// `packages/circ-policy`'s `BLOCK_CODE` is COMPUTED — `evaluateBlocks` derives
/// `TOO_MANY_LOANS` from the state and the policy every time it is asked, and
/// stores nothing. These are the STORED ones: a reason that outlives the
/// request, that a librarian can see on the patron's screen, and that a nightly
/// sweep maintains.
///
/// The three that overlap (`too_many_overdues`, `fine_limit_exceeded`,
/// `card_expired`) are the ones a library wants to SEE before the patron reaches
/// the desk, so the sweep materialises them; the resolver still computes its own
/// answer at checkout and the two agree because both read the same policy. The
/// rest have no computed equivalent at all.
enum PatronBlockCode {
  /// A librarian's own. `patron_blocks.reason` carries the sentence.
  manual
  too_many_overdues
  fine_limit_exceeded
  card_expired
  /// A notice came back undeliverable. A library that keeps posting to a dead
  /// address is a library that never gets its books back.
  address_unconfirmed
  /// The person has not returned a long-overdue item and the library has stopped
  /// lending pending a conversation.
  items_long_overdue
  lost_card

  @@map("patron_block_code")
}

/// Whether a message has been seen, and by whom it was meant to be seen.
enum PatronMessageAudience {
  /// Shown to staff at the desk when the patron is looked up.
  staff
  /// Shown to the patron in the OPAC. Phase 32 renders it; phase 14 stores it,
  /// because a message a library writes today should not have to be re-typed
  /// when the OPAC ships.
  patron

  @@map("patron_message_audience")
}

/// What the library does with a borrowing history once the book comes back.
///
/// §3: "Reading history is anonymised on return by default … This is an
/// IFLA/NISO professional obligation and a Greek DPA answer, and it is a DEFAULT
/// rather than a setting someone forgot to turn on."
///
/// `none` is not weaker than `anonymised`, it is stronger: it means the loan row
/// keeps no patron link even for the duration of the loan's statistics window.
/// School libraries default to it because a fourteen-year-old's reading is the
/// most sensitive list in the building.
enum ReadingHistoryMode {
  /// The link is severed on return. The statistical buckets survive.
  anonymised
  /// Kept, because the patron asked. Phase 32 owns the asking.
  kept
  /// Never linked.
  none

  @@map("reading_history_mode")
}

/// What a `loan_events` row records.
///
/// INVENTED. §3 names `loan_events` in the Circulation table list and gives it
/// no DDL; `effective_at` appears exactly once in the whole plan of record, in
/// the phase-16 line. So every value here is a decision, and the rule for
/// choosing them is the one phase 15 used for `item_status_reasons`: declare
/// what this phase WRITES, not what a later one might.
///
/// Phase 21 widens it — declare-lost, mark-found, claims-returned,
/// claims-never-borrowed, recall, repolicy (§6 phase 21, plus the
/// `POST /circulation/loans/repolicy` route §3 names as "writing a `loan_event`
/// per loan"). `ALTER TYPE … ADD VALUE` is a one-line migration, so pre-declaring
/// six values nothing writes would buy nothing and would make the enum a list of
/// intentions rather than a list of facts.
enum LoanEventKind {
  checked_out
  renewed
  returned
  /// The link to the patron being severed. §3: "`loans.patron_id` is nulled and
  /// `anonymised_at` stamped IN THE SAME TRANSACTION as the return." The event
  /// is written because the severing is a thing that happened to the loan and a
  /// library has to be able to show that it happened — but note the event
  /// carries no patron id either, so the record of the erasure is not itself a
  /// re-identification.
  anonymised

  @@map("loan_event_kind")
}

/// What a hold is a request FOR.
///
/// INVENTED. §3 names `holds` and gives it no DDL; its one paragraph
/// (MASTER-ARCHITECTURE.md:415) says "title / volume / item level" and stops, so
/// these three values are the whole of what the plan of record settles.
///
/// The three are NOT queue partitions — a bib has ONE queue and all three levels
/// share it, ranked by `queue_position`. The level is an ELIGIBILITY FILTER
/// applied when a returned copy looks for a hold to fill: a title hold takes any
/// copy, a volume hold takes any copy of that enumeration, an item hold takes
/// exactly one. The acceptance criterion settles it — "five mixed holds … fill
/// exactly three" only counts to five if the five share one sequence.
enum HoldLevel {
  /// Any copy of the bibliographic record. The ordinary request.
  title
  /// Any copy carrying this enumeration — "volume 3", "1998". A serial run is
  /// one bib and forty physical things, and a reader wants one of them.
  volume
  /// This copy and no other. `hold_policies.item_level_holds` decides whether a
  /// reader may ask for it at all.
  item

  @@map("hold_level")
}
